; ★★★THE PRIMITIVE IDS ARE A DISPATCH INDEX, NOT ONLY A NAME (2026-08-07).
; They still follow `primitive.h`'s DEFPRIMITIVE order — except for the
; twenty-four marked `hot-dispatch renumber` below, which were pulled down into
; 0..24 (11 stays one of the six historic holes) so that `Primitive.dispatch`
; can lower its `if` chain to a JUMP TABLE. Those twenty-four carry 94 % of the
; primitive calls of the heaviest codegen, 99 % of the single-file one and 96 %
; of a styling run; scattered ids give a compare chain instead and the whole
; split is then worth nothing (measured twice one level up, on `Insn.execute`).
; Each renumbered constant carries its old value in a trailing comment, so the
; mapping back to primitive.h is on the line itself. **Moving one of the
; twenty-four out of 0..24, or giving a new primitive an id inside that range
; without adding its arm to the shell, silently costs the jump table.**
;
; ★The six holes (11, 62, 63, 73, 101, 185 — names the reference never
; defines) were kept unreused so a stale prim_id in an old object could not
; alias a live primitive. That argument does not survive a renumbering and
; never applied to this build anyway: the engine is one whole-program LTO link
; out of one source tree, and nothing persists a prim_id across builds.
define PRIM_CONS: int 17   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 0
define PRIM_LIST: int 15   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 1
define PRIM_CAR: int 0   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 2
define PRIM_CDR: int 1   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 3
define PRIM_IS_NULL: int 2   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 4
define PRIM_IS_PAIR: int 25   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 5
define PRIM_IS_LIST: int 26   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 6
define PRIM_LENGTH: int 39   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 7
define PRIM_APPEND: int 51   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 8
define PRIM_REVERSE: int 53   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 9
define PRIM_LIST_REF: int 55   ; hot-dispatch renumber, was 10
; ★11 is a HOLE: the port used to install `memq`, which primitive.h has no
; entry for. So are 62/63 (char->integer, integer->char), 73
; (vector-length) and 101 (process-node) below — four more names the
; reference never defines. Removed 2026-07-31; the ids are left unreused so
; a stale prim_id in an old object can never alias a live primitive.
